var tafsirData_83 = {
  "1": "The sūrah opens with a devastating declaration: “Woe to the defrauders!” The word “Woe” translates the Arabic wayl, a severe expression of threat, punishment, and destruction. In the Qur’ān, wayl is used to warn of an impending, terrible chastisement. It is as if a valley of suffering in Hell is designated for these specific sinners.\n\nThe term “defrauders” translates al-muṭaffifīn, from the root ṭaffafa, which refers to something small, slight, or marginal. The muṭaffif is the one who deals in the tiny, barely noticeable fractions when measuring or weighing, but those tiny fractions are stolen from others. This is the sin of cheating in trade, of giving less than due in a way that is easily overlooked. The sūrah is Meccan, and while its immediate context addresses the dishonest commercial practices prevalent in Mecca, the condemnation is universal and timeless.\n\nThe one who shortchanges others, whether in the market, in the workplace, or in any trust, falls under this devastating “woe.” What makes the verse so powerful is that it begins with the threat without any introductory phrase. It plunges directly into the warning, showing the severity of the crime. The defrauder thinks his small manipulation goes unnoticed, but the Lord of all worlds opens a sūrah with a curse directed squarely at him.\n\nThis teaches a profound lesson: social justice and honesty in trade are not peripheral matters in Islam; they are central to faith. A person may pray, fast, and perform pilgrimage, but if he habitually cheats others in his business dealings, he is inviting wayl upon himself. The verse also implicitly answers those who deny the Judgment. The defrauder cheats because he does not truly believe he will be held to account.\n\nThe surah will go on to connect the cheating with the denial of the Day of Reckoning, showing that economic injustice is ultimately a spiritual disease.",
  "2": "Verse two precisely defines the defrauders by describing their behaviour: “Those who take full measure when they buy from people.” The Arabic phrase is alladhīna idhā iktālū ‘alā al-nāsi yastawfūn. The verb iktālū means they measure for themselves, taking by a measuring container. When they are the recipients, when they are buying and it is their turn to receive the goods, they demand full and exact measure.\n\nThe phrase ‘alā al-nāsi indicates that this measuring is to their own advantage and at the expense of others. The word yastawfūn means they take in full, they exact their right completely, they make sure not a grain is missed. This verse paints a picture of a person with a double standard. When it is their right to receive, they are meticulous, demanding, and exacting.\n\nThey ensure the scale is tipped in their favour down to the last ounce. There is no thought of generosity or leniency. This behaviour, on its own, is not necessarily criminal—people have a right to receive what they paid for. The crime is revealed in the next verse, where their behaviour when they are the givers is described.\n\nHowever, this verse already hints at the selfishness and the absence of God-consciousness that characterises the defrauder. His entire mindset is built on maximising personal gain. The use of the phrase “from people” (al-nās) generalises the victims to all of humanity, indicating that the defrauder’s greed is indiscriminate. He does not care who the buyer is; his only concern is his own profit.\n\nThis emphasis on the meticulousness of receiving serves to magnify the ugliness of the contrasting behaviour in the next verse. It sets up the psychological profile of the cheater: a person whose scales have two different standards, one for himself and one for others. This is the essence of injustice, and it stems from a heart that does not fear the ultimate accountability.",
  "3": "The crime is fully exposed in verse three: “but give less when they measure or weigh for buyers.” The Arabic wa idhā kālūhum aw wazanūhum yukhsirūn uses the verbs kālū (they measure) and wazanū (they weigh) to cover all forms of trade. The key word is yukhsirūn, which means they cause loss, they diminish, they give short. When the defrauder is the seller, and it is his turn to give the goods, he tips the balance the other way.\n\nHe skimps, he shaves off a little from the measure, he manipulates the scale so that the buyer receives less than what they paid for. The loss may be so small that the buyer does not notice, but it is theft nonetheless. This is the core of taṭfīf: stealing through tiny, almost imperceptible adjustments. The contrast with the previous verse is glaring.\n\nFor himself, he takes full measure; for others, he gives short. This is the hypocritical character of the unrepentant cheater. The verse reveals that his heart is not governed by a sense of justice or by the fear of Allah, but by a raw, calculating greed. The fact that the sūrah opens with such a specific social and economic sin shows the profound concern of Islam for fair dealing.\n\nThe marketplace is not a zone of moral exemption; it is a place where faith is tested and demonstrated. The Prophet (ﷺ) himself was known before his prophethood as al-Amīn, the trustworthy, in his trade dealings. He would later say that the honest trader will be with the prophets, the truthful, and the martyrs on the Day of Judgment. Conversely, the one who cheats is called out with wayl.\n\nThe verse also addresses a psychological reality: the defrauder often minimises his sin, thinking, “It’s just a little bit.” The Qur’ān calls it wayl, a tremendous destruction, because it is not about the quantity stolen but about the corruption of the soul, the violation of trust, and the denial of the coming Judgment. The entire social fabric unravels when such behaviour becomes widespread, which is why the warning is so severe.",
  "4": "After describing the crime, the sūrah immediately links it to its root cause in verse four: “Do such people not think that they will be resurrected?” The question is rhetorical and deeply probing. The Arabic alā yaẓunnu ulā’ika annahum mab‘ūthūn uses the verb yaẓunnu, which can mean to think, to consider, to have an opinion, or to be certain. Here, it carries the sense of a firm conviction that should be present.\n\nThe defrauders act as if they will never be brought back to life and held accountable. Their cheating is a practical denial of the resurrection. If they truly believed that they would stand before the Lord of all worlds and answer for every grain they shortchanged, they would not dare. The verse points to the direct relationship between belief in the Hereafter and ethical conduct.\n\nWhen belief in the resurrection weakens, corruption flourishes. When a person is certain that there is a Day of absolute justice where the tiny scales of this world will be re-weighed on the divine balance, then cheating loses its appeal. The profit gained by fraud is incredibly short-term; the punishment is eternal. The question “Do they not think?” is a challenge to the intellect.\n\nIt asks whether the defrauder has really considered the logical end of his existence. Has he thought about the graves spilling out, about standing alone with his record, about the scale that will weigh every atom of good and evil? The verse is an invitation to reflection, a call to connect the mundane act of measuring grain with the cosmic reality of the resurrection. It teaches that faith is not an abstract concept confined to the mosque; it is the invisible hand that should weigh every transaction.\n\nThe defrauder’s failure to think is not a lack of intelligence but a suppression of the truth, a deliberate turning away from the obvious conclusion so that he can continue his unjust gain. The following verses will describe the Day that he refuses to think about.",
  "5": "The Day that the defrauders fail to consider is now named in verse five: “for a tremendous Day.” The Arabic li-yawmin ‘aẓīm uses the adjective ‘aẓīm, which means great, tremendous, huge, awful, and of immense significance. This is the Day of Judgment. The word “tremendous” captures both the scale of the events that will unfold and the weight of the accountability that will take place.\n\nThe cheating that seemed so small and clever in the marketplace will be re-examined on a Day so vast that the heavens will split and the earth will be changed. The verse emphasises the total disparity between the petty crime and the cosmic setting of its reckoning. The defrauder tampered with a few ounces for a few coins of profit. He will face that action on the Day whose terror makes children grey-haired and whose length is fifty thousand years.\n\nThe word ‘aẓīm also conveys that the judgment of that Day is not a trivial matter. It will be conducted by the Lord of all worlds, and its outcome will be eternal bliss or eternal fire. The use of “tremendous” serves as a direct rebuttal to the defrauder’s mindset. He was concerned with tremendous profits in this world.\n\nThe verse tells him that the real tremendous thing is the Day he is ignoring. The description creates a sense of awe and fear, meant to shake the believer into rectifying his dealings and to warn the cheater of the impending disaster. The very fact that the Day is described as “tremendous” after the question about thinking implies that anyone who reflects on the nature of that Day cannot possibly continue in fraud. The tremendous Day dwarfs all worldly gains and makes them utterly worthless.\n\nThe believer is called to live with the ‘aẓīm constantly in mind, letting its grandeur restructure his priorities and his conduct in even the smallest of daily transactions.",
  "6": "Verse six provides the defining feature of this tremendous Day: “the Day all people will stand before the Lord of all worlds.” The Arabic yawma yaqūmu al-nāsu li-Rabbi al-‘ālamīn describes a universal standing. The verb yaqūmu means to stand, to rise, and to remain standing. All of humanity, from the first to the last, will be raised from their graves and made to stand in the court of the Almighty.\n\nThis standing is not a momentary one; it will last for a prolonged period while each person’s record is scrutinised, while the scales are balanced, and while the final decree is issued. The phrase “all people” (al-nās) includes the defrauders and their victims, the powerful and the weak, the rich and the poor, all in a single row, with no distinctions of worldly rank. The title “Lord of all worlds” (Rabbi al-‘ālamīn) emphasises the absolute sovereignty of the Judge. He is the Lord of every world, every realm, every creation.\n\nHis authority is not limited to a specific tribe, nation, or planet. The defrauder who acted as if he were a little lord over his own scale will now stand before the true Lord, the One who owns the very materials he weighed, the air he breathed, and the hands he used to cheat. This standing is the ultimate exposure. In the world, the cheater could hide behind his stall, manipulate in secret, and smile at his customers.\n\nOn that Day, he stands exposed, his entire record laid open, with no ability to cheat the divine scale. The verse connects the specific crime of defrauding to the universal Lordship of Allah. To cheat a human being is to rebel against the Lord of that human being and to violate the trust given by the Lord of all worlds. The verse is a powerful expansion of perspective.\n\nIt pulls the listener out of the narrow confines of the market and places him in the vast, terrifying court of the Day of Judgment, forcing the ultimate question: if you must stand before the Lord of all worlds, how will you weigh your actions today?",
  "7": "The sūrah now transitions from the general warning to the specific fate of the wicked, beginning with verse seven: “But no! The wicked are certainly bound for Sijjîn in the depths of Hell.” The opening “But no!” (kallā) is a strong negation, as if to say, “The matter is not as they think; they will not escape accountability.” “The wicked” translates al-fujjār, the same term used in the previous sūrahs for those who break moral boundaries.\n\nThey are now identified with the defrauders and those who deny the Judgment. Their destination is “Sijjîn,” a mysterious and ominous word. The verse itself explains it as being in the depths of Hell. Linguistically, Sijjîn comes from the root sijn, meaning prison.\n\nIt is a place of confinement, a narrow, oppressive dungeon within the Fire. It is the opposite of ‘Illiyūn, the elevated paradise of the righteous, which will be described later. The phrase “certainly bound for” translates la-fī, “indeed in,” with the particle la- giving emphasis. The record of the wicked is placed in Sijjîn, meaning their deeds are stored in this lowly, confined register, and they themselves will be cast there.\n\nThe verse reveals that the defrauding and denial are not random acts; they are symptoms of a deep-rooted wickedness that has a specific, designated punishment. The mention of Sijjîn creates a sense of dread and curiosity. What is this Sijjîn? The listener’s mind is captured.\n\nThe following verse will heighten the mystery by asking a rhetorical question. The verse also establishes a clear moral geography of the Hereafter: there is an elevated paradise and a deep, deep prison. The defrauders, who thought they were cleverly climbing the ladder of wealth, are in reality descending into the deepest confinement. The stark contrast between their worldly arrogance and their eternal humiliation is a key theme of this sūrah.",
  "8": "The terror of Sijjîn is amplified by the rhetorical question in verse eight: “and what will make you realize what Sijjîn is?” The Arabic wa mā adrāka mā Sijjīn is the same powerful device used in the previous sūrah for the Day of Judgment. It indicates that Sijjîn is a reality so severe, so beyond normal human comprehension, that words cannot fully convey its horror. The question is addressed to the Prophet (ﷺ) and through him to all listeners, drawing them into a state of awe and apprehension.\n\nIt is as if the Qur’ān is saying, “Whatever you might imagine Sijjîn to be, the reality is far worse.” The question also serves a rhetorical purpose: it pauses the description, allowing the dread to sink in before the answer is given. The word Sijjīn itself has been interpreted by scholars in several complementary ways. Some say it is the name of the lowest earth, the seventh and deepest level of Hell.\n\nOthers say it is a rock or a well in Hell. Yet others say it is the register or book in which the deeds of the wicked are recorded, a book that is placed in a confined, despicable place. All these meanings share the concept of extreme lowness, confinement, and disgrace. The question “What will make you realize?” implies that no human teacher, no book, no description can fully make one realise; only witnessing it firsthand would suffice, and by then it is too late.\n\nThe believer is thus urged to take the warning seriously, to live in such a way as to never have to find out what Sijjîn is. The verse is a masterpiece of psychological impact, using suspense and the limits of language to instil a deep-seated fear of the destination of the wicked, motivating the heart to flee from the sins that lead there.",
  "9": "The answer to the suspenseful question comes in the brief, ominous statement of verse nine: “a fate already sealed.” The Arabic kitābun marqūm literally means “a written book, inscribed, marked.” This refers to the record of the wicked that is placed in Sijjîn. The word kitāb means a book or a written record.\n\nThe word marqūm from the root raqama means something that is written in a clear, marked, and indelible manner. It is not a draft; it is a final, sealed document. Every deed of the fujjār is inscribed in this book, a record that cannot be altered, erased, or appealed. The “fate already sealed” indicates the finality of the judgment.\n\nThe defrauders may have thought their clever manipulations went unrecorded, but they were being marked down with absolute precision by the honourable angel-scribes mentioned in the previous sūrah. The book in Sijjîn is the compilation of their deeds, and it testifies against them. Some scholars also interpreted kitābun marqūm as meaning that the fate of the wicked is written and decreed by Allah’s knowledge, or that Sijjîn itself is a place where their punishment is written and prescribed. The overall meaning is one of inescapable destiny.\n\nThe defrauder’s life, his cheating, his denial, all of it has been sealed into a record that will determine his eternal abode. This verse is deeply sobering. It teaches that every action is being recorded, and the final “sealing” of the record at death marks the end of the opportunity to change. While alive, the book is still being written, and repentance can erase sins and replace them with good deeds.\n\nBut once the book is sealed, there is no return. The sealed fate of the wicked is a powerful call to ensure that one’s own book is being filled with deeds worthy of ‘Illiyūn rather than Sijjīn.",
  "10": "The theme of warning returns with renewed intensity in verse ten: “Woe on that Day to the deniers.” The word “Woe” (wayl) echoes the opening of the sūrah, creating a structural ring. The defrauders were given wayl at the beginning, and now the deniers are given wayl specifically “on that Day.” The phrase “on that Day” (yawma’idhin) anchors the threat to the Day of Judgment, the tremendous Day when people stand before the Lord of all worlds.\n\nThe “deniers” (al-mukadhdhibīn) are those who persistently deny the truth of the revelation, the resurrection, and the judgment. This verse explicitly connects the social crime of defrauding with the theological crime of denial. The defrauders are also deniers, as the earlier rhetorical question indicated: they do not think they will be resurrected. Their cheating is a form of practical denial.\n\nSo the woe that was announced for their fraud is now confirmed for their denial on the Day when denial becomes impossible. The verse serves as a refrain, punctuating the description of the wicked’s fate. It also shifts the temporal focus: the first wayl was a present warning, but this wayl is located on the Day itself, when the threat becomes reality. The deniers who laughed at the warnings will experience the woe firsthand.\n\nThe repetition of “woe” is a rhetorical device to keep the listener’s heart in a state of fear and reflection. It is as if the sūrah is saying: do not forget the central message; the cheater, the denier, the scoffer will all face the same devastating cry of destruction on that Day. For the believer, the repeated “woe” is a shield, a reminder to check one’s own faith and conduct so as not to fall among those who earn this terrible exclamation of punishment.",
  "11": "Verse eleven defines the deniers more precisely: “those who deny Judgment Day!” The Arabic alladhīna yukadhdhibūna bi-yawmi al-dīn identifies the specific object of their denial: the Day of Recompense. This is the same yawm al-dīn that was emphasised in the previous sūrah. The deniers reject not just any doctrine but the core reality that gives moral weight to human actions.\n\nBy denying Judgment Day, they render all their worldly dealings a game without ultimate consequences. This is the root that allows the defrauder to cheat: if there is no final court, no divine scale, no Hellfire, then the only smart thing to do is to maximise worldly gain by any means. The verse exposes the ideological foundation of corruption. The Meccan pagans did not deny the existence of Allah; many of them acknowledged a supreme Creator.\n\nBut they denied the resurrection and the judgment because it imposed moral accountability on their desires and commercial practices. The verse shows that belief in the Last Day is not an optional extra in faith; it is the pillar that upholds justice. Without it, the entire edifice of ethics collapses. The description “those who deny Judgment Day” is presented as an explanation for their state as “deniers” in the previous verse.\n\nIt is as if the sūrah answers the question: what are they denying? They are denying the very Day that this sūrah has been describing, the Day of standing before the Lord of all worlds. The verse also implies the absurdity of their position. They deny a Day whose signs are manifest in creation and whose necessity is written in the human conscience.\n\nTheir denial is not based on evidence but on a willful rejection of the truth that would force them to change their fraudulent ways. The believer, upon reading this, should feel a renewed conviction in the reality of the Judgment and a renewed commitment to living each day in its light.",
  "12": "Verse twelve provides a profound psychological and moral analysis of the denier: “None would deny it except every evildoing transgressor.” The Arabic wa mā yukadhdhibu bihī illā kullu mu‘tadin athīm uses a structure of exclusivity. The denial of Judgment Day is not an innocent intellectual error; it is the specific symptom of a corrupted character. The verse lists two qualities: mu‘tadin, meaning a transgressor, one who goes beyond the bounds, who is aggressive and unjust; and athīm, meaning a habitual sinner, one deeply immersed in evil.\n\nThe word “every” (kullu) indicates that these traits are universal among the deniers. This is a devastating diagnosis. The reason a person denies the Day of Reckoning is that he wants to continue his transgression without facing the consequences. Denial is a cover for sin.\n\nThe human intellect, left to its pure nature, recognises the need for ultimate justice and the logic of resurrection. But when a person indulges in persistent sin, he begins to hate the idea of judgment. He convinces himself it is not real so that he can enjoy his evil in peace. The verse thus flips the denier’s supposed intellectual superiority on its head.\n\nThe denier prides himself on being rational, free from superstition. The Qur’ān declares that his denial is actually the product of his moral failure. He is a mu‘tad, one who crosses limits, and an athīm, one steeped in sin. This connection between vice and disbelief is a crucial Qur’ānic theme.\n\nIt teaches that a sound heart leads to sound belief, and a corrupt heart leads to the rejection of truth. For the believer, this verse serves as a warning to guard one’s character. Persistent sin, if not repented from, can darken the heart and eventually lead to doubts and denial. It is also a call to see through the facade of the disbeliever’s intellectual objections and recognise the moral struggle underneath.\n\nThe verse thus links the earlier commercial fraud to a general lifestyle of transgression, showing that all of it flows from the same poisoned well: the refusal to accept accountability.",
  "13": "Verse thirteen gives a concrete example of the deniers’ attitude: “Whenever Our revelations are recited to them, they say, ‘Ancient fables!’” The Arabic asāṭīru al-awwalīn means the tales, myths, or writings of the ancient people. This was a common Meccan accusation against the Qur’ān. They claimed it was nothing new but merely a collection of old stories from previous civilisations that the Prophet (ﷺ) had learned and was passing off as divine revelation. The use of “Whenever” (idhā) indicates this was their habitual, knee-jerk reaction.\n\nWhenever the powerful verses about resurrection, judgment, Paradise, and Hell were recited, they immediately dismissed them as fairy tales. This dismissal served a dual purpose for them: it relieved them of the duty to reflect seriously on the message, and it protected their egos from admitting that an unlettered man from their own midst could bring a truth they had not discovered. The phrase “Our revelations” (āyātunā) personalises the offense. They were not rejecting the words of a human being; they were mocking the signs of Allah.\n\nThe accusation of “ancient fables” is a rejection of history as a source of wisdom. The Qur’ān frequently recounts the stories of past prophets and destroyed nations as lessons. The deniers tried to neutralise these lessons by relegating them to the status of folklore. The verse exposes the arrogance and intellectual dishonesty of the deniers.\n\nInstead of engaging with the content, they resorted to name-calling. This is a pattern that repeats in every age: when people find the divine message too demanding, they try to discredit it with labels—“myth,” “fairy tale,” “outdated.” The believer is comforted by this verse, knowing that the Prophet faced the same mockery. The appropriate response is not to despair but to continue conveying the message, knowing that the accusation of “ancient fables” is itself an ancient fable of the deniers across time.\n\nThe next verse will reveal the true internal reason for this dismissive reaction.",
  "14": "The sūrah now reveals the hidden reality behind the deniers’ mockery in verse fourteen: “But no! In fact, their hearts have been stained by all the evil they used to commit!” The opening “But no!” (kallā) again forcefully rejects their claim. The Qur’ān is not ancient fables; the real problem lies within themselves.\n\nThe Arabic word rāna means a covering, a stain, a rust that coats and envelops something. The phrase bal rāna ‘alā qulūbihim mā kānū yaksibūn means that their hearts have been covered, rusted, and stained by the sins they used to earn. This is one of the most profound psychological insights of the Qur’ān. Sins are not just external actions; they leave a residue on the heart.\n\nOver time, as sins accumulate without repentance, this residue forms a thick layer that blocks the light of truth. The heart becomes insensitive, unable to perceive the divine message as anything other than a stale myth. The Prophet (ﷺ) elaborated on this concept in a well-known ḥadīth: “When a believer commits a sin, a black dot appears on his heart. If he repents, gives up the sin, and seeks forgiveness, his heart is polished clean.\n\nBut if he increases in sin, the dot increases until it covers his entire heart. That is the rān which Allah mentions.” This verse thus explains why two people can hear the same Qur’ānic recitation and react completely differently. One heart is polished and receptive; the other is stained and repelled.\n\nThe deniers’ mockery is not a sign of their intellectual superiority but of their spiritual sickness. The verse is a powerful call to self-purification. Every believer must constantly examine their heart for the stains of sin and polish it with repentance. The phrase “all the evil they used to commit” indicates that the staining is a gradual, cumulative process.\n\nNo one becomes a denier overnight; it is the result of repeated sinful choices that slowly darken the soul. This awareness should inspire a vigilant and proactive approach to spiritual hygiene, ensuring that the heart remains a clear mirror for the light of revelation.",
  "15": "The consequence of the heart’s staining is revealed in verse fifteen: “Undoubtedly, they will be sealed off from their Lord on that Day.” The Arabic kallā innahum ‘an Rabbihim yawma’idhin la-maḥjūbūn uses multiple emphasis particles (inna, la) to make the statement absolutely certain. The word maḥjūbūn comes from ḥajaba, meaning to veil, to screen off, to prevent access. On the Day of Judgment, the deniers will be veiled, blocked, and prevented from seeing their Lord.\n\nThis is the ultimate deprivation. In contrast, the believers will have the supreme joy of looking upon their Lord in Paradise. The deniers, who in this world turned away from the remembrance of Allah, will find themselves turned away from His mercy and His vision on that Day. The phrase “from their Lord” is particularly poignant.\n\nHe is their Lord, their Creator, their Sustainer, but they rejected Him. Now the relationship is one of separation and veil. This verse is one of the primary scriptural proofs for the doctrine that the believers will see Allah in the Hereafter. The fact that the wicked are explicitly veiled indicates that the righteous are not veiled; they are granted the vision.\n\nThe scholars of the Salaf (the pious predecessors) were unanimous on this point. The verse also connects back to the stained hearts. The stain that covered their hearts in this world becomes a veil between them and their Lord in the next. The moral and spiritual blindness they cultivated by their sins becomes a literal screening off from the source of light and mercy.\n\nThis is the greatest loss imaginable. For the believer, this verse inspires a profound longing to be among those who are not veiled, to purify the heart so that it may one day witness the Countenance of the Most Generous. It also serves as a sobering warning against the sins that stain the heart, for those stains may become eternal barriers.",
  "16": "The deprivation of the vision of the Lord is not the only punishment. Verse sixteen adds: “Moreover, they will surely burn in Hell.” The Arabic thumma innahum la-ṣālū al-jaḥīm uses thumma to indicate an additional, sequential punishment. First, they are veiled from their Lord, and then they are made to enter and burn in the blazing Fire.\n\nThe word “burn” (ṣālū) means to enter into and be roasted by the fire. It is a direct, physical punishment that accompanies the spiritual punishment of being veiled. The Fire is al-jaḥīm, the intensely hot, raging Fire. The use of “surely” (la-) again emphasises the certainty of this outcome.\n\nThere is no escape, no alternative, no last-minute reprieve. The verse makes clear that the deniers will experience both the external torment of Hell and the internal torment of separation from Allah. Neither will be lightened. This dual punishment is the just recompense for a life spent in denial and defrauding others.\n\nIn the world, they sought to maximise their physical pleasure and wealth at the expense of their spiritual well-being. In the Hereafter, they will have nothing but physical pain and spiritual deprivation. The verse also serves to further refute the deniers’ claim that the Qur’ān is ancient fables. The Fire they mocked will become their undeniable reality.\n\nThe description is blunt and unadorned, leaving no room for reinterpretation. For the believer, this verse intensifies the motivation to avoid Hell at all costs. It also provides a sense of justice: the defrauders who seemed to get away with their cheating, and the deniers who seemed to enjoy their mockery, will face a punishment that perfectly matches their crimes. The burning in Hell is the final destination for those whose hearts were stained and who refused to believe in the Day of Reckoning.",
  "17": "The punishment is made complete by the verbal rebuke in verse seventeen: “and then be told, ‘This is what you used to deny.’” As the deniers are burning in Hell and veiled from their Lord, they will hear these words of excruciating reproach. The phrase “this is what you used to deny” connects the present suffering directly to their past attitude. The Hellfire that they are experiencing is the very thing they mocked and called “ancient fables.” The verse uses their own denial as a weapon against them.\n\nThere is no argument they can make, no appeal they can file, because they were warned repeatedly and they chose to scoff. The passive “be told” implies that angels, or Allah Himself, will deliver this statement, adding to their humiliation. This is the ultimate psychological torment layered on top of the physical burning: the crushing regret of knowing that they could have avoided this by simply believing. In the world, they demanded proof.\n\nNow the proof is surrounding them, consuming them, and the memory of their former arrogance burns hotter than the flames. The verse also serves a profound purpose for the living. It warns that the words we speak carelessly today—our denials, our mockery, our dismissals of the truth—may be played back to us on a Day when silence will be impossible. The believer should learn from this to guard the tongue and to never mock any aspect of the religion.\n\nThe verse is also a powerful tool for da‘wah. It invites the listener to imagine that very moment, to hear those words in their mind, and to choose to believe and reform before it is too late. The surah thus completes the portrait of the wicked’s fate with this final, piercing note of regret. The next section will shift dramatically to the opposite fate, the bliss of the virtuous, providing the full picture of divine justice.",
  "18": "With the same “But no!” (kallā) that introduced the wicked’s fate, the sūrah now turns to the destiny of the righteous in verse eighteen: “But no! The virtuous are certainly bound for ‘Illiyūn in elevated Gardens.” This parallel structure is deliberate and beautiful. Just as the fujjār are in Sijjīn, the abrār are in ‘Illiyūn.\n\nThe word “‘Illiyūn” comes from the root ‘alā, meaning to be high, elevated, sublime. It is the exact opposite of Sijjīn. If Sijjīn is the lowest of the low, the deep dungeon of Hell, then ‘Illiyūn is the highest of the high, the loftiest station in Paradise. The verse itself explains it as being “in elevated Gardens,” meaning the highest levels of Paradise.\n\nThe phrase “certainly bound for” (la-fī) again uses emphasis, making it as certain as the fate of the wicked. The “virtuous” (al-abrār) are the righteous, the pious, those who fulfilled their duties to Allah and to their fellow human beings. They are the opposite of the defrauders and the deniers. Instead of cheating in trade, they were honest; instead of denying the Judgment, they lived in its light; instead of staining their hearts with sin, they polished them with obedience.\n\nTheir book of deeds is placed in ‘Illiyūn, a record of honour and elevation. The contrast between Sijjīn and ‘Illiyūn represents the ultimate separation of humanity based on their deeds. The believer, upon reading this, is filled with hope and aspiration. The way to ‘Illiyūn is open, and it is paved with faith, honesty, and righteousness.\n\nThe verse also serves as a powerful argument for justice: the same divine system that casts the wicked into the deepest prison elevates the virtuous to the highest honour. The structural mirroring of the sūrah teaches that every human being is heading towards one of these two destinations.",
  "19": "The same rhetorical device used for Sijjīn is used for ‘Illiyūn in verse nineteen: “and what will make you realize what ‘Illiyūn is?” The Arabic wa mā adrāka mā ‘Illiyūn creates a sense of wonder and majesty. Just as the horrors of Sijjīn were beyond full human comprehension, so too are the delights and honours of ‘Illiyūn beyond what any eye has seen, any ear has heard, or any heart has imagined. The question draws the listener into a state of eager anticipation.\n\nThe use of the same question for both destinations highlights the perfect balance of the sūrah. The listener is made to ponder both extremes, to feel the dread of one and the longing for the other. The word ‘Illiyūn itself has been interpreted by scholars as the highest place in Paradise, the abode of the prophets, the martyrs, and the most righteous. Some say it is the name of the register where the deeds of the pious are recorded, kept in the highest heavens, witnessed by the angels nearest to Allah.\n\nOthers say it is the seventh heaven, or directly beneath the Throne. All these interpretations converge on the idea of supreme elevation, honour, and proximity to Allah. The question “What will make you realize?” reminds the believer that no description in this world can truly capture the reality of ‘Illiyūn. The pleasure of Paradise is a secret known only to Allah and those who enter it.\n\nThis unknowability is part of the motivation; the believer strives not for a fully comprehended reward but for a reward that is promised to be beyond all expectations. The question serves to elevate the listener’s gaze from the dusty scales of the marketplace to the sublime heights of the unseen, where the honest trader’s record rests in a place of unimaginable honour.",
  "20": "The parallel with the description of Sijjīn continues in verse twenty: “a fate already sealed.” The Arabic is exactly the same phrase used for the wicked: kitābun marqūm. But the context transforms its meaning. For the virtuous, the sealed book is not a record of doom but a record of honour, a document of confirmed salvation.\n\nTheir deeds are written, sealed, and preserved in the highest register. The “sealing” here implies finalisation and security. It means that the reward of the abrār is guaranteed, fixed, and cannot be lost or diminished. While the sealing of the wicked’s book brought despair, the sealing of the righteous’ book brings eternal reassurance.\n\nThe word kitāb can also refer to the entire decree of their blessed fate. It is inscribed, marked, and witnessed. This verse, like its counterpart for the wicked, emphasises the precision and finality of divine justice. Every honest transaction, every act of patience, every prayer, every tear of repentance is marked in that elevated book.\n\nNothing is lost. The defrauder’s clever manipulations were also marked, but in the book of Sijjīn. The believer should find immense comfort in this. The small, unseen acts of goodness, the secret charity, the honest word when a lie would have been profitable, the extra effort to give full measure—all of it is written in a book that resides in ‘Illiyūn.\n\nThe verse serves as a powerful motivator to fill one’s book with deeds that deserve such a sublime placement. It also provides a satisfying narrative closure: the defrauder’s double standard in measuring is answered by the divine double standard in recording—a despicable record in the deep prison or an honoured record in the highest paradise.",
  "21": "The description of the sealed fate of the virtuous continues in verse twenty-one, adding a dimension of exalted witness: “witnessed by those nearest to Allah.” The Arabic yashhaduhu al-muqarrabūn refers to the angels who are brought near to Allah, the highest-ranking celestial beings who are closest to the divine presence. This statement indicates that the register of the righteous, their kitāb marqūm placed in ‘Illiyūn, is not a hidden, obscure document. It is an open record witnessed by the most noble of creation, the angels of the highest heavens.\n\nThe word muqarrabūn comes from qaruba, to be near, and denotes those who have attained the supreme station of nearness to Allah. They include the great angels like Jibrīl, Mīkā’īl, Isrāfīl, and the bearers of the Throne. The fact that they witness the record of the abrār means that the honour of the virtuous is celebrated in the highest assembly. Their good deeds are not just recorded and stored; they are presented, acknowledged, and appreciated in the heavens.\n\nThis is an immense source of motivation for the believer. The small, honest act of giving full measure in a dusty market is seen and witnessed by the angels who are closest to the Lord of all worlds. It completely re-frames the value of righteous action. What seemed insignificant in the eyes of the defrauders is of such great worth that the muqarrabūn are called to witness it.\n\nThis also reinforces the concept of the communion of the righteous: the believers on earth are linked to the heavenly assembly, and their deeds rise to be witnessed by the purest beings. The verse also implicitly contrasts the two records: the wicked’s record is in the deep, dark Sijjīn, isolated and cursed, while the virtuous’ record is in the elevated ‘Illiyūn, surrounded by the light of the nearest angels. It is a powerful image of honour, transparency, and the ultimate recognition that awaits those who are faithful in the smallest of matters. The believer should strive to have their name mentioned in that noble gathering, witnessed by those nearest to Allah.",
  "22": "Having established the honoured place of their record, the sūrah now directly states their condition in verse twenty-two: “Surely the virtuous will be in bliss.” This is the same declaration found in Sūrah al-Infiṭār, and it serves as a repeated, comforting refrain. The Arabic inna al-abrāra la-fī na‘īm uses the emphatic particles inna and la- to leave no doubt. The abrār, the same righteous ones whose record is in ‘Illiyūn, will be immersed in na‘īm, which is pure, unadulterated bliss and delight.\n\nThis word encompasses every form of pleasure, joy, and comfort. It is not merely the absence of pain but a positive, overflowing state of happiness. The verse functions as a summary statement of the destiny of the virtuous. In the context of the surah, it provides the starkest contrast with the fate of the wicked.\n\nThe defrauders who took a little extra in the scales and denied the Judgment will be burning in Hell, while those who were honest and mindful will be in bliss. The present tense “will be” indicates the certainty of this future state, as if it is already a reality. For the early Meccan Muslims who faced economic boycott and cheating at the hands of the pagan defrauders, this was a profound consolation. Their patience and honesty were not going unnoticed; they were purchasing an eternal bliss that no amount of worldly profit could compare to.\n\nThe verse also speaks to the universal human desire for happiness. Everyone seeks bliss, but many seek it in the wrong places—in wealth, status, or fleeting pleasures. The verse redirects the seeker to the true and lasting bliss, which is only attained through faith and righteousness. It is a call to re-evaluate one’s definition of success.\n\nThe believer, upon reading this, should feel a deep sense of peace and a renewed commitment to the path of the abrār, knowing that their destination is na‘īm.",
  "23": "The bliss of the virtuous is given a vivid, sensory dimension in verse twenty-three: “seated on canopied couches, gazing around.” The Arabic ‘alā al-arā’iki yanẓurūn paints a picture of serene luxury and contemplative enjoyment. The word arā’ik is the plural of arīkah, which refers to a throne-like couch that is raised and covered with a canopy. It is a seat of honour, comfort, and privacy.\n\nThe believers in Paradise will recline on these magnificent couches, experiencing a state of complete relaxation and regal ease. The second part, “gazing around” (yanẓurūn), is a beautiful detail. They will look around at the delights of Paradise, at the beautiful gardens, at the rivers flowing, at the wonderful companions, and, most supremely, at the countenance of their Lord. The gaze is not one of anxiety or searching but of leisurely, joyful contemplation.\n\nThis is the reward for the believers who in the world would look at the signs of Allah with reflection and who would gaze upon the plight of the poor with compassion. Their sight in Paradise will be a source of continuous delight. The mention of canopied couches also evokes a sense of permanence and security. They are not merely sitting on the ground; they are enthroned, shaded, and honoured.\n\nThe word “seated” implies that the toil and struggle of the world are over. Now is the time of rest and enjoyment. This verse also implicitly contrasts with the description of the wicked, who will be burning in Hell with no comfort. The believers will be gazing around in wonder, while the disbelievers will be veiled from their Lord, unable to see anything that brings them joy.\n\nFor the believer, this verse creates a powerful visual anchor. When the trials of life weigh heavily, one can recall this image of reclining on a canopied couch in Paradise, gazing at the endless beauty that Allah has prepared. It instills hope and patience, transforming the hardships of the world into stepping stones to that eternal seat of honour.",
  "24": "Verse twenty-four adds another sensory detail that reveals the inner state of the believers in Paradise: “You will recognize on their faces the glow of delight.” The Arabic ta‘rifu fī wujūhihim naḍrata al-na‘īm uses the verb ta‘rifu, “you will recognize” or “you will know.” The “you” here addresses the Prophet (ﷺ) and every onlooker, emphasising that the joy of the believers is not hidden; it radiates visibly. The phrase naḍrata al-na‘īm is rich in meaning.\n\nNaḍrah refers to a radiant glow, a bright freshness, a lustre and beauty that shines from the skin. It is the visible effect of inner happiness, peace, and well-being. Al-na‘īm is the bliss mentioned in the previous verse, the source of this glow. So their faces will shine with the very bliss they are experiencing.\n\nThis is the opposite of the dusty, gloomy faces of the wicked described in Sūrah ‘Abasa. In this world, a person’s face may be marked by worry, fatigue, sadness, or the consequences of sin. In Paradise, the faces of the righteous will be illuminated with a light that reflects the eternal joy within. This verse connects outward beauty with inward purity.\n\nThe believers spent their lives purifying their hearts from the stains of sin, as earlier mentioned, and now that inner purity manifests as an outer radiance. It also indicates that in Paradise, there is no envy, no hidden sorrow, no masks. The joy is so complete that it shines through every face. For the believers who were mocked by the disbelievers, as the later verses will describe, this verse is a powerful reassurance.\n\nThe same faces that were looked upon with scorn in the world will glow with a delight that their mockers can only look upon with envy and regret. This verse encourages the believer to seek that inner light now, through sincere worship and good character, so that it may be perfected in the Hereafter. It is a vision of beauty that makes the fleeting pleasures of sin fade into insignificance.",
  "25": "The description of the pleasures of Paradise continues with a specific gift in verse twenty-five: “They will be given a drink of sealed, pure wine.” The Arabic yusqawna min raḥīqin makhtūm indicates that the believers in Paradise will be served a special drink. The word raḥīq refers to the purest, most refined wine, free from any impurity. It is described as “sealed” (makhtūm), meaning that it is kept in sealed containers, a sign of its preciousness and purity.\n\nIn this world, valuable drinks and perfumes are sealed to preserve their quality and prevent tampering. The seal guarantees that the drink is untouched and of the highest grade. The believers in Paradise will be served this exquisite wine directly, as a mark of honour and hospitality from Allah. It is crucial to note that the wine of Paradise is entirely different from the intoxicating wine of this world.\n\nThe Qur’ān explicitly states elsewhere that the wine of Paradise does not cause headache, intoxication, or vain talk. It is pure pleasure without any negative consequence. The word “sealed” also carries a connotation of exclusivity. This is a drink reserved for the honoured guests of the Most Merciful.\n\nSome scholars have even interpreted the seal as musk, as the following verse will mention the musk-like aroma at the final sip. The act of being given this drink signifies that the believers are in a state of celebration, a divine banquet where they are served by the best of hosts. For the early Muslims who were forbidden from drinking the intoxicating wine of this world, this verse offered a far superior alternative, one that they could anticipate with eagerness. It teaches the principle of delayed gratification: giving up a prohibited, harmful pleasure in this life for a pure, exquisite pleasure in the eternal life.\n\nThe believer is trained to look beyond immediate gratification and to trust that what Allah has in store is infinitely better.",
  "26": "The excellence of this paradisiacal wine is further described in verse twenty-six: “whose last sip will smell like musk.” The Arabic khitāmuhu misk refers to the seal or the final impression of the drink. When the drink is finished, the last drop or the lingering scent in the cup and on the breath will be that of pure musk. Musk is an intensely fragrant, precious substance used in perfumes, highly valued in Arabian culture.\n\nInstead of the foul smell associated with worldly alcohol, the heavenly wine leaves behind a delightful perfume. This sensory detail adds to the overall atmosphere of purity and refinement. Some scholars also interpret khitām as the seal of the container itself, meaning the wine is sealed with musk, indicating its supreme value. Both interpretations point to the exquisite nature of the reward.\n\nThe verse then adds a powerful call to action: “So let whoever aspires to this strive diligently.” The Arabic wa fī dhālika fa-l-yatanāfasi al-mutanāfisūn means “and for that, let the competitors compete.” The word yatanāfasi comes from nafāsah, which implies a competition in something precious, a rivalry in seeking something highly desirable. The verse transforms the description of bliss into a motivation for effort.\n\nThe delights of Paradise are not obtained by wishful thinking; they require striving. This striving is both internal—against the desires of the ego—and external—in acts of worship and honesty. The believer is called to compete not for the fleeting gains of this world, as the defrauders do, but for the eternal delights of the next. The word “strive diligently” captures the sense of a race, a determined and focused effort.\n\nThe verse connects back to the opening of the surah: while the defrauders compete to steal a few ounces in the marketplace, the believers should compete to earn the wine of Paradise sealed with musk. This is a profound redirection of human ambition. Ambition is not condemned; it is simply redirected to its proper, eternal object. The believer should be a fierce competitor for goodness, not for the trinkets of the world.\n\nThis verse serves as a timeless motto for the spiritual life.",
  "27": "The source of the exquisite drink is revealed in verse twenty-seven: “And this drink’s flavour will come from Tasnîm.” The Arabic wa mizājuhu min tasnīm indicates that the sealed wine is blended with water from Tasnîm, a special spring in Paradise. The word mizāj means mixture, blend, or the component that gives the drink its distinctive flavour. Tasnīm is the name of a specific spring, derived from the root sanama, which means to be high, elevated.\n\nIt is a spring that flows from the highest heights of Paradise. The fact that the wine of the abrār is mixed with water from Tasnīm shows that even their drink has a connection to the most elevated realms. The flavour and quality of a drink often come from the water source used. Tasnīm provides the purest, most delightful water, enhancing the raḥīq to a level of perfection.\n\nThe mention of Tasnīm introduces a hierarchy within the pleasures of Paradise. While the virtuous enjoy this wine mixed with Tasnīm, the following verse will explain that those nearest to Allah drink from Tasnīm directly. This indicates that the abrār, the general righteous, receive a taste of the higher blessings through mixture, while the muqarrabūn, the foremost, receive them in their purest form. It is a beautiful picture of interconnected bliss and graded reward.\n\nThe believers are all in bliss, but their ranks vary according to their striving. The verse motivates the believer to not just seek entry into Paradise but to aspire for the highest ranks, to be among those who drink directly from Tasnīm. The name Tasnīm itself, with its connotation of height and elevation, reminds the believer that the path to this spring is through raising one’s spiritual station in this world. The verse adds yet another dimension to the reward, making the heart yearn for that blessed water and the effort required to reach it.",
  "28": "The full status of Tasnīm is clarified in verse twenty-eight: “a spring from which those nearest to Allah will drink.” The Arabic ‘aynan yashrabu bihā al-muqarrabūn identifies Tasnīm as a spring reserved primarily for the muqarrabūn, those brought near to Allah. This is the same group mentioned in verse twenty-one, the highest-ranking angels who witness the record of the righteous, and, as used here, the highest class of believers—the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs, and the most righteous. They will drink directly and purely from Tasnīm, without it being mixed with anything.\n\nThe abrār, the general righteous, will have their wine blended with it, receiving an overflow of the blessings granted to the muqarrabūn. This beautiful hierarchy shows the interconnectedness of the inhabitants of Paradise. The higher ranks receive the purest essence, and the lower ranks partake of its flavour through the generosity of Allah. It is not a source of envy but of harmony and shared delight.\n\nThe verse also teaches that nearness to Allah is the ultimate achievement. The muqarrabūn are not necessarily those with the most wealth or fame in the world; they are those who excelled in faith, sincerity, and good deeds. They drew near to Allah through their devotion, and their reward is to drink from the spring of nearness. For the believer, this verse is both a comfort and a challenge.\n\nIt comforts by showing the abundance of Allah’s mercy, where even the general righteous taste Tasnīm. It challenges by setting a high bar: strive to be muqarrab, strive to drink directly. The mention of those nearest to Allah also brings the surah’s theme of spiritual vision full circle. The wicked were veiled from their Lord, while the muqarrabūn, who drink from Tasnīm, enjoy the highest degree of nearness and, as other verses indicate, the vision of the divine countenance.\n\nThe verse concludes the description of the rewards, leaving the listener with a profound longing for the upper reaches of Paradise, and the understanding that the path begins with the small choices of integrity and faith in the marketplace.",
  "29": "With the rewards and punishments fully described, the sūrah now turns to a specific historical scene, the moral psychology of which provides deep satisfaction. Verse twenty-nine begins: “Indeed, the wicked used to laugh at the believers.” The Arabic inna alladhīna ajramū kānū min alladhīna āmanū yaḍḥakūn uses the past tense “used to” (kānū), indicating a habitual action in the worldly life. The “wicked” are the mujrimūn, the criminals, those who committed the crimes of disbelief, fraud, and mockery.\n\nTheir crime included laughing at the believers, ridiculing their faith, their poverty, and their commitment to honesty. This verse references the experience of the early Muslims in Mecca, where the powerful pagan elite would mock the weak and oppressed who embraced Islam. Figures like Bilāl, ‘Ammār, and Ṣuhayb were laughed at by the nobles of Quraysh for abandoning the idols and following an unlettered prophet. The laughter was a weapon of humiliation, designed to make the believers feel small and foolish.\n\nThe verse acknowledges the pain of that mockery by recording it in the divine revelation. It validates the believers’ suffering and tells them that their Lord has seen and heard everything. The choice of “the wicked” (alladhīna ajramū) instead of simply “the disbelievers” links their mockery to their criminal nature. Mocking the faithful is a crime against dignity, a sin that will be answered.\n\nThis verse also reveals a timeless pattern: the people of falsehood often use ridicule as their tool when they cannot defeat the truth with reason. The believer is thus forewarned and comforted. The laughter of the wicked is not a sign of their strength but of their arrogance, and it will be answered. The use of “Indeed” (inna) assures the listeners that this is a factual account, a record that will be presented on the Day of Judgment, where the roles will be reversed.",
  "30": "The description of the mockery becomes more specific and vivid in verse thirty: “wink to one another whenever they passed by.” The Arabic wa idhā marrū bihim yataghāmazūn uses the verb yataghāmazūn, which means to wink, gesture with the eyes, or nudge each other in a secretive, mocking manner. As the believers would walk by, the wicked would catch each other’s eyes and exchange knowing, contemptuous glances. This is a form of subtle, psychological bullying.\n\nThe wink conveys a message: “Look at these fools. How can they believe in resurrection? How can they give up the pleasures of life for some imaginary paradise?” It is a gesture designed to belittle and isolate the believers, to make them feel like outcasts in their own city.\n\nThe verse captures the arrogance and the group mentality of the wicked. They reinforce each other’s mockery through these non-verbal cues, creating a hostile social environment. The phrase “whenever they passed by” shows that this was a continuous, relentless behaviour. Every encounter was an opportunity for mockery.\n\nThis historical detail would resonate deeply with the early Muslims, who endured this silent torture daily. By recording this wink in the Qur’ān, Allah exposes the hidden meanness of the disbelievers. That secret wink, which they thought no one of consequence noticed, is seen by the Lord of all worlds and is written in the eternal record. The verse serves as a powerful reminder that no act of cruelty, no matter how small or subtle, escapes divine notice.\n\nThe wink that was meant to shame the believer will shame the winker on the Day of Judgment. For believers in any age who face similar quiet mockery—the raised eyebrow, the sarcastic glance, the condescending smirk—this verse provides immense comfort. The One who sees all will bring justice, and the winks will be answered.",
  "31": "The pattern of the wicked’s behaviour continues in verse thirty-one: “and muse over these exploits upon returning to their own people.” The Arabic wa idhā inqalabū ilā ahlihimu inqalabū fakihīn describes the wicked returning to their families and friends after their encounters with the believers. The word inqalabū means they turned back, they returned. The word fakihīn (also read as fakihīn) means jesting, amusing themselves, being in a state of playful, mocking delight.\n\nSo they would go home and recount their day’s “exploits”—how they mocked this believer, how they winked at that one, how they cleverly cheated in a trade, how they laughed at the idea of resurrection. The mockery was not just a public performance; it was a source of private entertainment and family bonding. They would sit with their wives and children and turn the believers into objects of ridicule, laughing together at the “fools” who had abandoned the religion of their ancestors. This verse reveals the depth of their heedlessness.\n\nTheir social life was built around the mockery of the truth. The word fakihīn can also imply a sense of self-satisfaction and complacency. They returned home pleased with themselves, convinced of their own cleverness and the stupidity of the believers. The verse sets up the ultimate reversal.\n\nThe home, which was the site of their arrogant laughter, will be replaced by the Fire. The family, with whom they shared their cruel jokes, will be of no help. The believer, who may have been the subject of those evening mockery sessions, can find solace in knowing that the divine record preserves every word and every laugh. This verse also serves as a warning to believers about the type of entertainment they enjoy.\n\nAre our gatherings filled with mockery of others, or with remembrance of Allah? The wicked’s “musing” led to their doom; the believer’s gatherings should be led to nearness to the Lord.",
  "32": "The verbal form of their mockery is recorded in verse thirty-two: “And when they saw the faithful, they would say, ‘These people are truly astray.’” The Arabic wa idhā ra’awhum qālū inna hā’ulā’i la-ḍāllūn uses the emphatic la- to stress their claim of misguidance. The “faithful” are the believers, those who had embraced Islam. The wicked, looking at the believers, pronounced them to be profoundly lost. This is the classic inversion of reality by the arrogant.\n\nThe believers had found the Straight Way, the path to eternal bliss and ‘Illiyūn, yet the disbelievers called them astray. The believers had abandoned idol worship, cheating in trade, and the immoral practices of the pre-Islamic society. From the perspective of the Quraysh elite, this abandonment was a deviation from the norm, a loss of social standing and economic advantage. So they labelled it “ḍalāl,” misguidance.\n\nThis verse captures the loneliness of the truth-bearer in a corrupt society. When the majority has normalized falsehood, the one who adheres to the truth appears strange and lost. The believers were called astray because they refused to defraud, because they believed in the resurrection, because they worshipped one God. The verse consoles the believers that this accusation is a badge of honour.\n\nThe prophets before them were called the same. It also exposes the psychology of the wicked: they must convince themselves and others that the righteous are astray in order to justify their own sinful lifestyle. If the believers are astray, then the wicked do not have to examine their own hearts. This accusation is a shield against the call of truth.\n\nFor the believer today, this verse is a reminder that being called “astray” by a world that has lost its moral compass is a sign that one might be exactly on the Straight Path. The final reversal in the surah will show who is truly astray when the ultimate reality is unveiled.",
  "33": "The verse immediately comments on the absurdity of the disbelievers’ judgmental stance: “even though they were not sent as keepers over the believers.” The Arabic wa mā ursilū ‘alayhim ḥāfiẓīn states that the wicked were never appointed as guardians, watchers, or keepers over the believers. The word ḥāfiẓīn means those who guard, watch over, and hold responsibility. The disbelievers had no divine mandate to judge the spiritual state of the believers.\n\nTheir pronouncement of “These people are astray” was not based on any authority or knowledge; it was pure arrogance and presumption. They were not prophets, nor angels, nor anyone entrusted with the guidance of others. They were simply fellow human beings, and sinful ones at that. This verse strips them of their assumed role as judges.\n\nIt implies that the real judge is Allah, and He has already declared the believers to be on the Straight Path and the wicked to be in error. By mocking and judging the believers, the wicked were usurping a role that belongs to Allah alone. This is a profound lesson for all believers. We are not to be overly concerned with the judgments and mockery of people who have no authority to judge.\n\nOur worth is not determined by the winks and whispers of those who reject the truth. The only opinion that ultimately matters is that of the Lord of all worlds. Furthermore, the verse hints that the disbelievers, by acting as self-appointed keepers, were actually exposing their own insecurity. They needed to put the believers down to feel secure in their own path.\n\nThe believer is liberated from the tyranny of public opinion by this verse. It does not matter that the world calls you astray; what matters is that Allah calls you guided. The disbelievers were not sent as keepers, so their judgment is null and void. The only Keeper whose judgment counts will be revealed on the tremendous Day.",
  "34": "Now comes the great reversal, the moment of divine justice that forms the climax of the sūrah. Verse thirty-four declares: “But on that Day the believers will be laughing at the disbelievers.” The Arabic fa-al-yawma alladhīna āmanū min al-kuffāri yaḍḥakūn uses “that Day” (al-yawma) to refer emphatically to the Day of Judgment. The particle fa- (but/so) connects the past mockery to the future recompense.\n\nThe very believers who were laughed at, winked at, and called astray will be the ones laughing. This laughter is not the cruel, mocking laughter of the wicked in the world; it is a laughter of relief, of justice, of witnessing the truth they held onto being vindicated. The believers will look upon the disbelievers in their state of punishment—burning, veiled, humiliated—and they will laugh. This is not an act of petty revenge but a divinely sanctioned response that restores the moral balance.\n\nThe ones who were humiliated for their faith are now honoured, and they are given the joy of seeing their oppressors receive their just deserts. This verse provides immense comfort to the oppressed faithful. It assures them that the roles will be completely reversed. The laughter of the believers is part of their bliss.\n\nIt is not that they are devoid of mercy; rather, they are rejoicing in the perfection of divine justice. The disbelievers laughed in arrogant denial; the believers laugh in grateful witness to the truth of Allah’s promise. The verse also serves as a warning to those who mock the believers today: your laughter is temporary, and it is being recorded. A day is coming when you will be the object of laughter, and that laughter will echo for eternity.\n\nThe believer should not despair at the mockery they face but should look forward to that Day with patient hope, knowing that the final laugh belongs to those who held onto the truth.",
  "35": "The scene of the believers’ laughter is placed in its full, majestic context in verse thirty-five: “as they sit on canopied couches, looking on.” The Arabic ‘alā al-arā’iki yanẓurūn is the exact same phrase used in verse twenty-three to describe the believers’ state in Paradise. Here, it is repeated to connect the laughter with the bliss. The believers are not standing in a crowd laughing; they are reclining on their thrones of honour, under canopies, in a position of absolute security and comfort.\n\nFrom this elevated station, they will “look on” (yanẓurūn) at the disbelievers. The verb yanẓurūn means to gaze, to observe, to contemplate. Their gaze is not a nervous glance but a steady, regal observation. They will see the hellfire, the punishment, the humiliation of those who wronged them, and this sight will increase their gratitude for the mercy of Allah.\n\nThe canopied couches emphasise the permanence of their state. They are not standing in a temporary court; they are already in their eternal abode, and they look out from Paradise to witness the fate of the wicked. This is the ultimate vindication. The same couches that symbolise their rest and reward become the seats from which they witness the fulfillment of divine justice.\n\nThe repetition of the phrase “canopied couches” creates a structural ring in the surah, linking the description of bliss with the description of the final triumph. The believer is meant to visualise this scene: the poor, oppressed, mocked Muslim of Mecca, now seated on a throne of unimaginable beauty, looking at Abu Jahl or Abu Lahab in the Fire. It is a vision that makes every worldly sacrifice seem trivial. The verse is a powerful motivator to endure hardship with patience, knowing that the couch is prepared and the day of looking on is certain.",
  "36": "The sūrah concludes with the final, satisfying note of justice in verse thirty-six: “The believers will be asked, ‘Have the disbelievers not been paid back for what they used to do?’” The Arabic hal thuwwiba al-kuffāru mā kānū yaf‘alūn is a rhetorical question posed to the believers. The passive “will be asked” implies that Allah, or the angels by His command, will direct this question to the believers, drawing their attention to the perfect recompense that has been delivered. The verb thuwwiba means to be paid back, to be rewarded or requited, with a full and appropriate compensation. The question “Have they not been paid back?” expects an affirmative answer: yes, indeed, they have been paid in full.\n\nEvery wink, every laugh, every act of fraud, every denial—all of it has been compensated with exact justice. The phrase “for what they used to do” (mā kānū yaf‘alūn) encompasses the entire catalogue of their crimes described in the surah: the defrauding in the scales, the denial of Judgment, the staining of hearts, the mockery of the believers. Nothing has been overlooked. This final question is a source of profound psychological relief for the believers.\n\nIt is as if Allah is personally reassuring them that the account is settled, that their pain has been witnessed and avenged, not out of pettiness but out of perfect justice. The question also implicitly contains a warning for any who might repeat the behaviour of the Meccan disbelievers. The sūrah, which began with “Woe to the defrauders,” ends with the confirmation that the woe has been executed, and the believers are invited to witness and affirm it. The believer, seated on the canopied couch, looking on, will answer from the depth of their heart: “Yes, they have been paid back, and all praise is due to the Lord of all worlds.”"
};
